   "format": "mcq",
   "stem": {
      "text": "The functions \\( f \\) and \\( g \\) are differentiable, with \\( f(1) = -4 \\) and \\( f'(1) = 4 \\). The line tangent to the graph of \\( g \\) at \\( x = 1 \\) has equation \\( y = 2 x \\). Let \\( h(x) = \\frac{f(x)}{g(x)} \\). Find the value of \\( h'(1) \\)."
   },
   "answer_key": {
      "form": "symbolic",
      "mathjson": 4
   },
   "worked_solution": [
      {
         "step": 1,
         "text": "Record the values at \\( x = 1 \\): \\( f(1) = -4 \\), \\( f'(1) = 4 \\). The tangent line gives \\( g(1) = 2 \\) (its height at \\( x = 1 \\)) and \\( g'(1) = 2 \\) (its slope).",
         "mathjson": 4
      },
      {
         "step": 2,
         "text": "By the quotient rule, \\( \\left(\\frac{f}{g}\\right)'(1) = \\frac{f'(1)g(1) - f(1)g'(1)}{[g(1)]^{2}} \\).",
         "mathjson": -4
      },
      {
         "step": 3,
         "text": "Substitute: \\( \\frac{(4)(2) - (-4)(2)}{(2)^{2}} = \\frac{16}{4} \\).",
         "mathjson": 4
      },
      {
         "step": 4,
         "text": "So \\( h'(1) = 4 \\).",
         "mathjson": 4
      }
   ],
